{"type": "thinking", "text": "OK so appending works. Deleting beyond the appended suffix doesn't. This is suspicious: maybe the backspace only removes characters typed in the current editing session? That is, the initial value is like a pre-filled immutable token. But then editing an amount would be impossible unless you clear... Hmm, but earlier $44 -> $4 worked, which was a digit from initial value. Wait, was $44 initial? No, $44 was created by typing. Right: original \"$40.04\" → after editing... so all previous \"44\" was typed in session.\n\nWait initially the field showed \"$40\". I typed \"45\" via type_text → \"$40.04\"?? That's confusing. Then erase -> \"$40\". So initial token \"40\" is immutable to backspace perhaps. Let me test: type \"5\" then erase -> \"40\"; erase -> \"40\". So yes, can't remove below initial value.\n\nHmm, but that would be a major usability bug (can't lower an amount). But maybe the amount is a custom calculator expression where the initial amount is displayed as a formatted result and the erase clears the appended part, and there's a \"clear\" action elsewhere (long press erase? or a C button). Let me check if long-press on erase clears all. Or maybe the initial text is selected so first keypress replaces (but our keypress appended, indicating NOT selected).\n\nWait, when I first opened the editor, the amount field was highlighted (green selection). Screenshot showed $40 highlighted with green background. That indicates text selected! Then typing \"45\" via type_text resulted \"$40.04\" — hmm, if selected, typing should replace. Unless type_text sends \"45\" via IME and the app doesn't handle IME replace. But tapping keypad \"5\" while selected appended \"$40.5\" — so selection not treated as replace by the custom keypad either. Hmm, but maybe the \"selection\" is just a focus highlight (whole field background green), not text selection. Yes! The green box behind $40 is focus highlight, not selection. OK.\n\nSo how do you clear? Maybe long-press erase. Let me long-press erase at (917,1077)."}
