{"type": "thinking", "text": "Rotation preserved scroll position and Week selection presumably.\n\nHmm, wait. Now I realize: in the savings card, \"Current savings 35%\" and \"Available: $35\" and \"Ideal savings: $20\". Maybe \"Current savings\" should be the amount saved so far = available? The \"35%\" is available. Eh.\n\nLet me consider that the savings card figure might be wrong after editing the expense. Before my edit (spend 60), available 40%. After edit (spend 65), available 35%. It updated. So \"computed together with other analytics data\" works.\n\nHmm. But maybe the bug is in the savings calculation when a recurring expense exists. Let's compute:\n- Total budget $100, spent $65 ($45 one-time + $20 recurring).\n- Ideal savings 20% of budget = $20.\n- Current savings 35% = $35 = 100 - 65.\n- Recurring expenses $20 (20%), One-time $45 (45%). Sum = 65%.\n- Remaining/available 35%.\n\nThat's all consistent. So the savings card seems fine. Unless \"Current savings\" should be 20%+something.\n\nMaybe the intended savings figure is the \"Current savings\" percentage computed as (budget - spent)/budget, i.e. 35%. Fine.\n\nLet's test analytics with a period selected via \"Past periods\" or \"New budget period\"? Not necessary.\n\nLet me test the chart \"Categories\" view and \"Total\" filter for consistency. Also the donut chart. In the screenshot, the donut has pink (One-time 45?) and purple (recurring 20). The legend \"Groceries $45\" and \"Rent $20\". Fine.\n\nLet's tap Categories tab and see the breakdown. Scroll up a bit (two swipes down from y=1900)."}
