Notifications, Data backup, App information. No budget. So the budget is only via the home pill.\n\nOK. Let me now think about the \"days remaining\" inconsistency more. Maybe report it. But first, let's hunt for a more clear-cut seeded bug in the expense editor, analytics savings, and budget settings.\n\nExpense editor: We tested amount, category/reason, date. What about time? Change time and see if saved. What about recurrence toggle? Let's test on Groceries: turn \"Recurrent Expense\" on, save, and see. That changes data (creates a recurring expense). We can turn it back. But recurring may create subscriptions; risky but reversible perhaps.\n\nWhat about the \"Purchase Reason\" free-text editing: we saw typing a reason then pressing the confirm button created/kept a category. This might be where the \"linked fields\" bug is: editing the reason might re-derive the category. Hmm.\n\nLet's test: In the editor, change the reason text to \"Coffee\" (a new custom reason) and save; then check history \"Purchase Reason Coffee\" and analytics category. If analytics shows Food still, then category isn't re-derived from the reason—consistent with \"stored as passed\"? But maybe expected to create a new category or assign to Food. Hard to judge.\n\nWait, maybe \"linked fields\" refers to the amount and the \"recurring\" series: when editing a recurring expense's amount, the app may re-derive the series' amount. Let's test by inspecting the \"Mark as paid\" / recurrence behavior. Actually we can't see the future series.\n\nHmm, let me re-read the note: \"History expense editor: edits are now stored as passed, without re-deriving linked fields.\" This is a developer note about a change. It implies the editor had a bug where edits were re-derivation-based. A QA tester should verify that edits (amount, reason, category, date, time, recurrence) are stored exactly as passed and linked fields (e.g., category derived from reason) aren't silently changed. We did verify amount/reason/date persist. \n\nMaybe the seeded bug is the opposite: the app now does NOT re-derive a linked field that it should. For example, editing the amount of an expense should NOT change its category. That works. Or editing the reason should update the displayed category... Hmm.\n\nLet me test the time change: change Groceries time from 12:00 to 08:00 and save, then check the row shows \"8:00 AM\". Then revert. This tests another field. Let's do it quickly. Currently the budget sheet is open; close it and open history.\n\nActually, we need to revert Rent to 20 and Groceries to $40 & 13 Nov anyway. Let's do that first, then test time change on Groceries maybe.\n\nLet's close the sheet (tap 0), then back to calculator, swipe down to history."}
