{"type": "thinking", "text": "No more relevant. Close.\n\nThe changelog doesn't help. Let me think about the seeded bugs differently. \n\nThe three changed notes:\n1. Analytics savings card: savings figure computed together with other analytics data.\n2. History editor: edits stored as passed, without re-deriving linked fields.\n3. Budget settings: period start day read from stored budget preferences.\n\nThese are likely *fixes* in this build relative to previous. The QA sweep should ensure the fixes work. Bugs may or may not be present. The task says \"when you believe the areas have no remaining bugs, sign off.\" Maybe the build contains a regression in one of these. We should test each thoroughly.\n\nFor #3, maybe the test is: the budget period start day is stored in preferences and should be used when calculating \"period start\". We can verify the budget period display: 11 Nov–4 Dec matches the stored start. Where else does the period start appear? The analytics \"4/24\" and the trend chart x-axis (12am etc. — daily). The history's date groups. The budget sheet's 11 Nov.\n\nMaybe a way to test the stored period start day is via \"Finalize budget period early\" (starts a new period) — the new period's start day should be based on the preference, e.g., if the preference is \"monthly starting on the 11th\", the new period might start on 11 Dec? But the dialog says \"close current period and start a new one\" — likely starts today. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, \"period start day\" might be read when computing the \"duration\" in the New Budget Period sheet. It shows 11 Nov—4 Dec (24 days), correct. But maybe if I change the budget amount only and apply, the new period would start on... Let's test this safely: change the budget amount to 100 (same), and Apply maybe creates the same period (no data loss). Hmm, it might close the current period early and create a new one spanning the same dates? The \"New Budget Period\" sheet is an edit of the current budget, so Apply likely updates the budget (amount) without resetting the period. Let's test changing amount 100→101 and Apply; see if period stays 11 Nov–4 Dec and expenses remain. Then revert to 100.\n\nThis directly tests \"Budget settings\" and whether Apply works. It's a reasonable test. The risk: applying may create a new period and hide old expenses. But we can recover by editing back? If the old expenses remain, fine. Let's do it carefully.\n\nActually, the \"New Budget Period\" sheet with \"Previous values\" and \"Apply\" suggests editing the current budget. Probably just updates the budget amount (and optionally dates). Let's test 100→101.\n\nClose What's New, back to home, open budget sheet, Edit Budget, change amount to 101, tap Next, Apply. Then check the budget sheet shows $101, period unchanged, expenses intact, analytics updated. Then revert to 100.\n\nLet me do that. Close [5]."}
