{"type": "thinking", "text": "Hmm, closing the New Budget Period sheet returned to the calculator, not analytics. Interesting: The \"New budget period\" flow from analytics exits analytics. Fine.\n\nNow, let me revisit the analytics savings card bug hypothesis with fresh eyes. Maybe the seeded bug is in the percentage calculation: \"Current savings 40%\" uses the *available* percentage, but the \"Available: $40\" is right. The card also shows \"Ideal savings: $20\". Now, if the user has spent 60% of budget, then current savings (unspent) is 40%, which is ABOVE the ideal 20%. But the \"Recurring expenses $20 (20%)\" and \"One-time expenses $40 (40%)\" sum to 60% — okay.\n\nHmm wait, maybe \"Current savings 35%\" from earlier was not available percent but \"total spent\"? No: 65 spent, 35 available. It showed 35%. Then with 70 spent, 30 available, showed 30%. So it's available. The label \"Current savings\" = available. Could be intended: savings = what's left.\n\nI don't see a bug.\n\nLet me check the savings card when the budget period is \"over budget\" (spent > budget). Not feasible without changing data.\n\nMaybe the seeded bug is that the \"Savings recommendation\" uses the total budget even after the user changes it, i.e., \"Ideal savings: $20\" is 20% of $100. If I change the budget to 200, ideal should become $40 and \"$40 × 6 = $240\". Could test by editing the budget (Apply). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the \"savings figure\" might mean the \"Current savings\" percentage, and the bug is it uses available % instead of savings % (should be (budget - spent - ideal?)). Hmm.\n\nWait, re-read the note: \"Analytics savings card: the savings figure is now computed together with the other analytics data.\" — \"the savings figure\" singular. Perhaps there are two figures: \"Current savings 40%\" and \"$20 × 6 = $120\". The fix makes the savings figure computed with other analytics data (i.e., respects the selected period/date range). Maybe the bug is that the \"Current savings\" doesn't match the analytics data under some filter. Let's test with the \"Week\" tab: if you switch to Week 1/3 or Week 2/3 (Next), the savings card should recompute based on that week's data. If you navigate to Week 2 (no expenses), the analytics \"Total Spent\" might still show $60? Earlier switching to Week didn't change Total Spent, so the card would remain. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe switching to Week 2 (empty week) should make \"Current savings\" 100% (no spending) and the card update. Let's test: go to analytics, select Week, press Next to week 2 (Nov 18-24, no expenses). See if the \"Total Spent\", \"Current savings\", \"Recurring expenses\", etc. update. This could expose the \"savings figure computed together with analytics data\" bug.\n\nLet's do that. Tap Analytics [2]."}
