{"type": "thinking", "text": "Aha! This is interesting: After editing the *current* Rent occurrence from $20 to $25, the future occurrence outside the period also changed to **$25** (\"Rent — In 4 weeks — $25\"). So the edit propagated to the recurring series. \n\nIs that a bug? The changed note says \"History expense editor: edits are now stored as passed, without re-deriving linked fields.\" Hmm. \"Stored as passed\" means when you edit an occurrence, the app stores the edited values as-is. \"Without re-deriving linked fields\" — the linked future occurrence's amount is a linked field; if the app re-derived it from the template, it would stay $20. But here it changed to $25, meaning the edit to the occurrence *did* propagate to the recurring series. That might be the intended behavior for recurring expenses (editing the series). Or it might be the bug: editing a single occurrence should only affect that occurrence.\n\nHmm, which is right? In many expense apps, editing a recurring expense asks \"this occurrence or all occurrences\". Here there's no choice. The \"Mark as paid\" suggests each occurrence is a separate transaction, but the future occurrence is an unpaid upcoming one. If you edit the current occurrence's amount, the future planned Rent amount changes too — maybe intended for a subscription whose price changed.\n\nActually, re-read the changed note: \"History expense editor: edits are now stored as passed, without re-deriving linked fields.\" Hmm, \"as passed\" suggests the editor passes updated fields to the DB. \"without re-deriving linked fields\" could mean the app no longer re-derives the *amount* of linked recurring occurrences from the template. If it's now fixed, editing the occurrence should NOT change the future occurrence. But we observed the future occurrence changed to $25! That would mean the bug is still present: the linked future Rent was re-derived from the edited amount.\n\nWait, but maybe the propagation is the *intended* behavior of a recurring expense: the series amount is the same field. Hmm.\n\nLet me test the reverse: edit the *future* occurrence? Can't; it's outside the period and shown in \"Show subscriptions\" list. Maybe tapping it opens something? The \"Rent — In 4 weeks\" row is clickable. Let's tap it to see. It may open the recurring payment management screen where you can edit the series. But the amount is now $25.\n\nHmm, let me think about what \"linked fields\" are. Could be:\n- The \"Purchase Reason\" (e.g., Rent) and the category (Food).\n- The recurrence link.\n- The date/time.\n\nIf editing a recurring occurrence's amount changed the future one, that's a link effect. But is it a bug? Let me test with the *reason*: change Rent's reason to \"Housing\" and see if the future occurrence's reason changes too. If all edits propagate, it's series editing. If the app is supposed to edit only the occurrence, this is the bug.\n\nBut which behavior is \"correct\" per the app's design? The recurring expense \"series\" concept: The future occurrence is generated from the series definition (amount, reason, date). The current occurrence is part of the series. Editing the occurrence might be intended to edit the series (common in subscription trackers). The note \"edits stored as passed, without re-deriving linked fields\" suggests the editor used to re-derive fields and store those instead of the passed values. E.g., if you edit the amount, the app would re-derive the *same* amount for the series or re-derive the category from the reason. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the seeded bug is exactly this: editing the *occurrence* amount should only change the occurrence, but instead it changes the recurring template/future occurrences. The note says edits are *stored as passed* (the occurrence's fields) *without re-deriving linked fields* (the series). So the future occurrence should remain $20. Observed $25 → bug!\n\nHmm, but \"stored as passed\" could also mean the series' amount is updated with the passed value. Ugh.\n\nLet me look for more evidence: The \"Rent\" expanded card shows \"Mark as paid\". If the series amount changed, the future \"In 4 weeks\" is $25. Also, if I now check analytics, recurring expenses would be $65, one-time $0. That's consistent with the period occurrence being $25.\n\nAnother way to test the \"linked fields\" behavior: edit the *date* of the current Rent occurrence from 13 Nov to 12 Nov. If the future occurrence's date shifts (e.g., next Rent in 4 weeks changes to a different date), then edits propagate to the series. But the upcoming date \"In 4 weeks\" is relative; hard to see the exact date. Hmm.\n\nWhat if editing the *reason* of a recurring Rent to \"Groceries\" changes the future occurrence's reason/amount? We can see the future row's label (\"Rent\" vs \"Groceries\"). That's a narrow signal. But again, propagation could be intended.\n\nLet me check the \"Show subscriptions outside the period\" row before/after: earlier it showed \"Rent — In 4 weeks — $20\". After editing the current occurrence to $25, it shows \"$25\". So the *series amount* is definitely shared.\n\nIs this a bug the task wants? The changed area explicitly says \"History expense editor: edits are now stored as passed, without re-deriving linked fields.\" The word \"linked fields\" strongly hints at linked recurring occurrences. If the app now stores the edit as passed, maybe the *occurrence* should show $25 but the linked future occurrence should stay $20. The observed propagation to $25 suggests the app updated the linked field — the opposite of the note. This is a promising seeded bug!\n\nBut wait: maybe the \"fix\" means the opposite: previously, editing an occurrence would re-derive (i.e., regenerate) the linked fields from the series, overwriting the edit. Now it's stored as passed, so the edit sticks. The future occurrence changing could be the \"linked field\" being derived from the edited occurrence, which might be a side effect... Hmm.\n\nLet me consider a cleaner interpretation with the editor's \"Purchase Reason\" field:\n- The expense's category (Food) is linked to the Purchase Reason (Rent → Food). Previously, the app would re-derive the category from the reason on edit. Now it stores the passed category.\n- When we changed the reason to FooGroceries, the category chip... hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the \"linked field\" could be the *category* linked to the *reason*. In the editor, when we selected \"Food\" (top-level), the reason became \"Food\" (it displayed \"Food\" in the history). When we typed \"FooGroceries\" and saved, would the category become the typed text? We didn't fully test saving a custom reason. Hmm.\n\nLet's think about the \"category concat when detected calculation\" changelog item! \"Category concat when detected calculation\" — maybe related to categories concatenated when detected. In the app, the \"Purchase Reason\" is free text, and the category is detected from it (e.g., \"Groceries\" contains \"Grocer\" → Groceries category). The changelog mentions \"Category concat when detected calculation\" — a feature where the category is concatenated when detected. Hmm.\n\nThe changed note \"edits are now stored as passed, without re-deriving linked fields\" might refer to this: when you edit an expense's amount, the app used to re-run category detection (re-derive the category from the reason), potentially overwriting the category. Now it stores what you pass. We tested amount edits and the reason/category stayed. So that's fine.\n\nThe fact that editing a recurring occurrence's amount updates the future occurrence might be an intended \"recurring series\" behavior, not \"linked fields\" in the note. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at whether there's a way to edit *just this occurrence* in the recurring config. The \"Configure recurrence\" sheet had a switch and schedule; no \"this occurrence only\" option. So editing the series is plausible design.\n\nHmm, I'm not confident. Let me consider the other possibility: editing the current Rent amount to $25 and seeing the future occurrence $25 might be correct *if* the app treats the recurring series amount as one field. Since the future occurrence is generated from the same series, it should share the amount. That's normal in subscription trackers. So not a bug.\n\nLet me revert Rent to 20 now. Then I'", "truncated": true}
